1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,873
The better question came from a pedestrian at a rural stop, late in the season.

2
00:00:04,873 --> 00:00:11,027
“Do you understand what a short walk to the station means here?” she asked.

3
00:00:11,027 --> 00:00:15,576
“In the countryside, a walk is not exercise.

4
00:00:15,576 --> 00:00:26,619
It is the whole approach to the world.” The team had a fact, a date, and a century of assumption, and none of them answered her.

5
00:00:26,619 --> 00:00:37,531
The royal reception, the agricultural bulletin, and the local championship had all been covered; the scholarship and the fellowship had not.

6
00:00:37,531 --> 00:00:41,925
“A partner is not a trainer,” the sailor said.

7
00:00:41,925 --> 00:00:55,372
“A trainee is not a medal.” Their uniform, their tune, and their romance with the city were the background of the report, and the report was flattering to the wrong people.

8
00:00:55,372 --> 00:01:06,135
The dramatic episode, when it came, was a telephone call: the sponsor had abandoned the membership list to a transmitter in the capital.

9
00:01:06,135 --> 00:01:10,419
“I speak for the pedestrian,” Aisha said.

10
00:01:10,419 --> 00:01:22,912
“He is alone, and he is not a cartoon.” It was a descriptive sentence, not a scholarly one, and it was the best line in the file.

11
00:01:22,912 --> 00:01:28,680
The false assumption was that a flourish in the figures meant a flourishing service.

12
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:34,724
“The companionship of the queue is not an instalment plan,” the trainer said.

13
00:01:34,724 --> 00:01:48,806
“You cannot steer a village with a spreadsheet.” The reserved young man from the lodge, the last sailor of the old line, and the manager of the reception desk all agreed with him.

14
00:01:48,806 --> 00:02:01,772
After the meeting, the team stopped writing about colonialism and began writing about the stop itself: who waited there, what they carried, and how long they had been waiting.

15
00:02:01,772 --> 00:02:07,416
It was certainly a smaller question, and it was finally the right one.

16
00:02:07,416 --> 00:02:14,919
They thanked the pedestrian, ran the numbers again through the month, and put her sentence at the top.

17
00:02:14,919 --> 00:02:22,283
After all, the date was far off, and it was likely that the matter would run into another month.

18
00:02:22,283 --> 00:02:27,996
Yesterday it had seemed an ordinary affair; today it was a dear one.

19
00:02:27,996 --> 00:02:34,900
“We must thank the training, and we must relate the new figure to the old,” she said.

20
00:02:34,900 --> 00:02:46,891
It was certainly the better question, and finally they could say so — an answer they had been walking through since the morning of the first day, for a century.
